# Issue #140 regression guard: the get-side clone must be a no-op for a primitive value and
# a correct owned-bit clone for a heap string value (built via interpolation). Both must get
# + free cleanly. Confirms emit_value_clone's passthrough and string branches.
# https://github.com/BigWhale/sushi-lang/issues/140
#
# THE LEAK ASSERTION BELOW IS DELIBERATELY RED. This file leaks 8 bytes in 1 block, and
# carried no `EXPECT_NO_LEAKS` at all until MM.md finding F9 was pinned, so the leak was
# recorded in prose only. Prose does not fail CI. Do not remove the directive to get the
# file green; the directive is the whole point of it.
#
# DIAGNOSED 2026-08-03, NOT FIXED. It is not a HashMap defect. Bisection removed the map
# entirely and the leak stayed:
#
#   fn go() string:
#       let string base = "there"
#       return Result.Ok("hi-{base}")
#   println(go().realise("err"))        # leaks 8 bytes -- exactly len("hi-there")
#
# An owned `string` that NO BINDING NAMES leaks when it is consumed straight in an
# argument position. Binding it first (`let string s = go().realise("err")`) frees it
# correctly. The same hole shows through `??` and through an extension method that returns
# an interpolated string, so it is general, not specific to `.realise()` or to this file.
#
# The language already has this rule for a method-call receiver: an owning temporary no
# binding names is registered as a scope temp and freed at scope exit. Strings have no
# such registry. `_string_cleanup` is keyed by NAME, and a temporary has none; closures
# solve it with a value-keyed `_closure_temp_cleanup` and C strings with `_cstr_cleanup`.
# A string equivalent needs the registry, its push/pop, its early-exit emitter, and a
# decision at every producing site about which string values are owned temps and which are
# borrows -- and that last question is exactly what the string tier answers.
#
# So this goes to Phase 9, which rewrites that machinery. Registering too eagerly trades
# the leak for a double free on any borrow-shaped string.
# EXPECT_RUNTIME_EXIT: 0
# EXPECT_STDOUT_EXACT: "5\nhi-there\n"
# EXPECT_NO_LEAKS: true
use <collections/hashmap>

fn get_num() i32:
    let HashMap@(i32, i32) nums = HashMap.new()
    nums.insert(1, 5)
    let i32 n = nums.get(1)??
    nums.free()
    return Result.Ok(n)

fn get_word() string:
    let HashMap@(i32, string) words = HashMap.new()
    let string base = "there"
    words.insert(2, "hi-{base}")
    # The get-out borrows what the map still owns, so `got` must be an independent value
    # before the map is freed and before it is returned.
    let string got = words.get(2)??.clone()
    words.free()
    return Result.Ok(got)

fn main() i32:
    println(get_num().realise(-1))
    println(get_word().realise("err"))
    return Result.Ok(0)
